          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"cofield-nikkis-touch-e622228c1fdb\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Keith's direct reply primarily avows as true that he reciprocates the addressee's love. “Too” makes the utterance responsive and reciprocal, while the steady, sincere delivery strongly favors a considered assertion over reflex-like expressive discharge. The exchange has minor relational-confirming force, but the excerpt does not establish a substantial commitment or undertaking.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The immediately preceding declaration is omitted, so the precise relationship and stakes are unavailable; this limits confidence about performative force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Keith directly says “I love you too” to an interlocutor in narrated dialogue.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Keith first stills and then smiles before answering. His reciprocating “too,” together with the narrator's explicit characterization of his voice as steady and sincere, presents the reply as a genuine, controlled avowal rather than an impulsive verbal discharge. It may reassure the interlocutor and affirm their relationship, but those effects do not by themselves make it a strong performative undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“I love you too,”\",\"supports\":\"The canonical love declaration strongly supports truth-conditional avowal; “too” marks reciprocal response to another declaration.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“his voice steady and sincere”\",\"supports\":\"The narration explicitly presents the avowal as genuine and controlled, supporting T and weighing against an expressive/reflex-like E reading.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Keith stilled, then smiled.”\",\"supports\":\"The pause-like bodily response and subsequent smile frame the declaration as affectively meaningful, but do not independently establish E or a substantial undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.86},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is located at relative position 0.618718 in Ania Cofield's Nikki's Touch; only an 85-character context window is supplied.\",\"supports\":\"The limited extraction explains why broader relational stakes and any stronger performative implications cannot be established from the supplied material.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the reciprocal avowal, with slight P support for relational affirmation. Delivery and sincerity are adequately represented as context, and no independent E or residual O dimension is needed.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
